display and quiz storage.\n\n\nThe dashboard's quiz names, seen as button labels, likely map to a `label` field within the Firestore document. The \"Rename\" button suggests updating this `label` field, effectively renaming the quiz's displayed title. Initial observation: the `label` appears to be the user-defined name. However, a potential inconsistency exists if there is a separate title field. Further investigation is needed to clarify the relationship between `label` (rename button) and an inner quiz title within the editor.\n\n\nThe quiz label serves as the display name within the dashboard, used in file naming, but distinct from the quiz title shown to students during the quiz. Ensuring preservation of `currentQuizLabel` and `currentQuizOrder` during save operations is crucial.  The dashboard should list quizzes using metadata from the `quizzes/{id}` documents: `label` (string) for display and `quizOrder` (integer) for correct sorting.\n\n\nThe system should provide default slots if Firestore has only the \"active\" quiz. These defaults should be merged with existing quiz documents, excluding the \"template\" quiz and sorted by `quizOrder`.  \"Add more Quiz\" functionality should compute the next available number, create a unique ID, set the label and order, and write or patch a lightweight document in Firestore.  The method used to fetch quiz metadata requires checking.\n\n\nFirestore `runQuery` is a strong candidate because the existing security rules allow read access to the `/quizzes` collection. This allows a projection for the dashboard to select only the `label`, `quizOrder`, and `title` fields.  The application uses an encrypted query format, obfuscating sensitive information like field names, but the essential structure of the query, including the `collectionId` and `select` fields, remains observable. The `runQuery` endpoint with a `structuredQuery` will be assessed using a read-only test before final implementation.\n\n\nFirestore's projection, lacking an `orderBy` clause, should minimize index requirements and filter out undesired documents. This approach should correctly handle missing fields (`label`, `quizOrder`) and exclude the \"template\" document.\n\nCritical issue: downloading quiz files directly to students! The logic in `index.html` at line 430 is crucial. A web link with `?quiz=quiz_2` works because the URL parameter is read. However, when a student downloads `Quiz_2.html` and opens the local file, the URL parameter is absent. This will not work if we do not update this quiz file's logic to handle both online and downloaded quiz views.\n\n\nCritical observation: When a quiz is downloaded, `window.location.search` is empty. The existing code uses this parameter to determine the quiz ID, leading to incorrect behavior for downloaded quizzes because they default to the \"active\" quiz. To correct this, the system needs to prioritize the quiz ID stored within the `#local-db-vault` element if present. If the element is not there, the system should follow existing behavior. This will ensure that both online and downloaded versions of a quiz operate correctly and do not share data incorrectly.\n\n\nThe immediate focus is on optimizing data fetching, specifically replacing the repeated `vEl.textContent.trim()` and `unsealPayload` calls with a single initialization of `vaultData`. Verifying the synchronization of the compiled `index.html` to `quizzes/template` is crucial to ensure standalone functionality for downloaded quiz files. The goal is that each downloaded quiz correctly connects to its specific `quizId`.\nNext, examining teacher interface navigation, and the precise wording of user requests will reveal if there is an important nuance about UI actions. The UI questions are where `teacher.html` first loads and the location of the `Dashboard` button.\n\n\nInitial hypothesis: dashboard navigation starts from `teacher.html`. If no `quiz` parameter is present, the dashboard view is loaded, displaying quiz buttons. The teacher may click \"Add more Quiz\" OR click \"Load Quiz\" to open the quiz editor. The `?quiz=quiz_2` parameter triggers direct loading of the quiz editor view. The UI should have a dashboard navigation button in both cases.\n\nIf `teacher.html` first loads, the focus shifts to how the dashboard is navigated. Is it an automatic display or through a specific action like an \"Add\" button?\n\n\nThe teacher interface appears to load the dashboard by default, unless a specific quiz ID is provided. Clicking \"Load Quiz\" or \"Add more Quiz\" actions dynamically affect the view. The user's goal is to ensure a clear \"Dashboard\" navigation button is always available, providing easy access to the quiz list. Considering this, the existing test behavior needs to be re-evaluated to ensure the default behavior is expected when `teacher.html` loads without a quiz ID.\n\n\nThe teacher interface, when loaded without a quiz ID, defaults to displaying the dashboard view. The UI should consistently present a \"Dashboard\" button, irrespective of how the interface is accessed or which view (dashboard or editor) is active. The \"Load Quiz\" button directly opens the editor, and \"Add more Quiz\" initiates quiz creation. There might be some questions about previous changes when the user writes the word \"reset.\"\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"@'\n(async () => {\n  const url = 'https://firestore.googleapis.com/v1/projects/sir-jasper-quiz/databases/(default)/documents:runQuery';\n  const body = {\n    structuredQuery: {\n      from: [{ collectionId: 'quizzes' }],\n      select: { fields: [{ fieldPath: 'label' }, { fieldPath: 'quizOrder' }, { fieldPath: 'title' }] }\n    }\n  };\n  const r = await fetch(url, { method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, body: JSON.stringify(body) });\n  const data = await r.json();\n  console.log('runQuery HTTP:', r.status, 'byteLength:', JSON.stringify(data).length);\n  console.log(JSON.stringify(data, null, 2));\n})();\n'@ | node -","WaitMsBeforeAsync":10000,"toolSummary":"Test Firestore runQuery projection on quizzes"}}]}
